::ALG2_U01_L03_Q01::
Question ID: ALG2_U01_L03_Q01
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.AE.1; MLA.A2.AE.5
Question:
Factor 8x³ - 12x².
{
~A. 4x(2x² - 3x) #This factors out 4x but not the greatest common factor.
=B. 4x²(2x - 3) #Correct. The greatest common factor is 4x squared.
~C. 2x²(4x - 6) #This is not fully factored because 4x - 6 still has a common factor of 2.
~D. 4x²(2x + 3) #The sign inside the factor should be negative.
}


::ALG2_U01_L03_Q02::
Question ID: ALG2_U01_L03_Q02
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.AE.1; MLA.A2.AE.5
Question:
Factor 12x² + 18x.
{
~A. 3x(4x + 6) #This is not fully factored because 4x + 6 still has a common factor of 2.
~B. 6x(2x - 3) #The sign should be positive because both original terms are positive.
~C. x(12x + 18) #This misses the numerical greatest common factor.
=D. 6x(2x + 3) #Correct. The greatest common factor is 6x.
}


::ALG2_U01_L03_Q03::
Question ID: ALG2_U01_L03_Q03
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.AE.1; MLA.A2.AE.5
Question:
Factor x² - 64.
{
=A. (x - 8)(x + 8) #Correct. This is x squared minus 8 squared.
~B. (x - 8)(x - 8) #This expands to x squared - 16x + 64.
~C. (x + 8)(x + 8) #This expands to x squared + 16x + 64.
~D. x(x - 64) #This expands to x squared - 64x.
}


::ALG2_U01_L03_Q04::
Question ID: ALG2_U01_L03_Q04
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.AE.1; MLA.A2.AE.5
Question:
Factor 4x² - 25.
{
~A. (2x - 5)(2x - 5) #This expands to 4x squared - 20x + 25.
~B. (4x - 5)(x + 5) #This does not multiply back to 4x squared - 25.
=C. (2x - 5)(2x + 5) #Correct. This is a difference of squares.
~D. (2x + 5)(2x + 5) #This expands to 4x squared + 20x + 25.
}


::ALG2_U01_L03_Q05::
Question ID: ALG2_U01_L03_Q05
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.AE.1; MLA.A2.AE.5
Question:
Factor x² + 10x + 21.
{
=A. (x + 3)(x + 7) #Correct. 3 and 7 multiply to 21 and add to 10.
~B. (x + 1)(x + 21) #This gives a middle term of 22x.
~C. (x - 3)(x - 7) #This gives a negative middle term.
~D. (x + 4)(x + 6) #This multiplies to 24, not 21.
}


::ALG2_U01_L03_Q06::
Question ID: ALG2_U01_L03_Q06
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.AE.1; MLA.A2.AE.5
Question:
Factor x² - 11x + 24.
{
~A. (x + 3)(x + 8) #This gives a positive middle term.
~B. (x - 4)(x - 6) #This gives a middle term of -10x.
~C. (x + 4)(x + 6) #This gives a positive middle term and the wrong middle coefficient.
=D. (x - 3)(x - 8) #Correct. -3 and -8 multiply to 24 and add to -11.
}


::ALG2_U01_L03_Q07::
Question ID: ALG2_U01_L03_Q07
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.AE.1; MLA.A2.AE.5
Question:
Factor x² - 3x - 28.
{
~A. (x + 7)(x - 4) #This gives a middle term of 3x.
=B. (x - 7)(x + 4) #Correct. -7 and 4 multiply to -28 and add to -3.
~C. (x - 14)(x + 2) #This gives a middle term of -12x.
~D. (x - 7)(x - 4) #This gives a positive constant.
}


::ALG2_U01_L03_Q08::
Question ID: ALG2_U01_L03_Q08
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.AE.1; MLA.A2.AE.5
Question:
Factor x² + 2x - 35.
{
~A. (x + 5)(x - 7) #This gives a middle term of -2x.
~B. (x - 7)(x - 5) #This gives a positive constant.
=C. (x + 7)(x - 5) #Correct. 7 and -5 multiply to -35 and add to 2.
~D. (x + 1)(x - 35) #This gives the wrong middle term.
}


::ALG2_U01_L03_Q09::
Question ID: ALG2_U01_L03_Q09
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.AE.1; MLA.A2.AE.5
Question:
Factor x² + 9x + 20.
{
~A. (x + 2)(x + 10) #This gives a middle term of 12x.
~B. (x - 4)(x - 5) #This gives a negative middle term.
~C. (x + 1)(x + 20) #This gives a middle term of 21x.
=D. (x + 4)(x + 5) #Correct. 4 and 5 multiply to 20 and add to 9.
}


::ALG2_U01_L03_Q10::
Question ID: ALG2_U01_L03_Q10
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.AE.1; MLA.A2.AE.5
Question:
Factor x² - 12x + 36.
{
=A. (x - 6)(x - 6) #Correct. -6 and -6 multiply to 36 and add to -12.
~B. (x + 6)(x + 6) #This gives a positive middle term.
~C. (x - 4)(x - 9) #This multiplies to 36 but gives a middle term of -13x.
~D. (x + 4)(x + 9) #This gives a positive middle term.
}


::ALG2_U01_L03_Q11::
Question ID: ALG2_U01_L03_Q11
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.AE.1; MLA.A2.AE.5
Question:
Which expression is a difference of squares?
{
~A. x² + 16 #This is a sum of squares, not a difference.
~B. x² - 10x + 25 #This is a perfect square trinomial.
=C. x² - 16 #Correct. This is x squared minus 4 squared.
~D. x² + 8x + 16 #This is a perfect square trinomial.
}


::ALG2_U01_L03_Q12::
Question ID: ALG2_U01_L03_Q12
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.AE.1; MLA.A2.AE.5
Question:
Which expression is fully factored?
{
~A. 2(x² - 9) #The binomial is still a difference of squares.
=B. 2(x - 3)(x + 3) #Correct. This is fully factored over the real numbers.
~C. x² - 9 #This expression is not factored.
~D. (2x - 6)(x + 3) #The first factor still has a common factor of 2.
}


::ALG2_U01_L03_Q13::
Question ID: ALG2_U01_L03_Q13
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.AE.1; MLA.A2.AE.5
Question:
Which expression is equivalent to (x + 4)(x + 6)?
{
=A. x² + 10x + 24 #Correct. Multiplying gives x squared + 6x + 4x + 24.
~B. x² + 10 #This misses the middle terms and has the wrong constant.
~C. x² + 24x + 10 #The middle coefficient and constant are switched.
~D. x² - 10x + 24 #The middle term sign is wrong.
}


::ALG2_U01_L03_Q14::
Question ID: ALG2_U01_L03_Q14
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.AE.1; MLA.A2.AE.5
Question:
Which factored form is equivalent to x² - 5x + 6?
{
~A. (x + 2)(x + 3) #This gives a positive middle term.
~B. (x - 1)(x - 6) #This gives a middle term of -7x.
=C. (x - 2)(x - 3) #Correct. -2 and -3 multiply to 6 and add to -5.
~D. (x + 1)(x + 6) #This gives a positive middle term.
}


::ALG2_U01_L03_Q15::
Question ID: ALG2_U01_L03_Q15
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.AE.1; MLA.A2.AE.5
Question:
Which factored form is equivalent to x² + x - 30?
{
~A. (x + 5)(x - 6) #This gives a middle term of -x.
~B. (x - 6)(x - 5) #This gives a positive constant.
~C. (x + 3)(x - 10) #This gives a middle term of -7x.
=D. (x + 6)(x - 5) #Correct. 6 and -5 multiply to -30 and add to 1.
}


::ALG2_U01_L03_Q16::
Question ID: ALG2_U01_L03_Q16
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.AE.1; MLA.A2.AE.5
Question:
What is the greatest common factor of 15x³ and 10x²?
{
~A. 5x #This is a common factor, but it is not the greatest common factor.
=B. 5x² #Correct. The coefficient GCF is 5 and the smallest power of x is x squared.
~C. 10x² #10 is not a factor of 15.
~D. 25x⁵ #This multiplies instead of finding a common factor.
}


::ALG2_U01_L03_Q17::
Question ID: ALG2_U01_L03_Q17
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.AE.1; MLA.A2.AE.5
Question:
Factor 15x³ - 10x².
{
~A. 5x(3x² - 2x) #This is not fully factored because x is still common inside.
~B. 10x²(5x - 1) #10x squared is not a factor of both terms.
=C. 5x²(3x - 2) #Correct. The greatest common factor is 5x squared.
~D. 5x²(3x + 2) #The sign inside should be negative.
}


::ALG2_U01_L03_Q18::
Question ID: ALG2_U01_L03_Q18
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.AE.1; MLA.A2.AE.5
Question:
Factor 2x² - 18 completely.
{
=A. 2(x - 3)(x + 3) #Correct. First factor out 2, then factor the difference of squares.
~B. 2(x² - 9) #This is not complete because x squared minus 9 is a difference of squares.
~C. (2x - 3)(x + 3) #This does not multiply back to 2x squared - 18.
~D. 2(x - 9)(x + 9) #This would multiply to 2x squared - 162.
}


::ALG2_U01_L03_Q19::
Question ID: ALG2_U01_L03_Q19
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.AE.1; MLA.A2.AE.5
Question:
Factor 3x³ - 12x completely.
{
~A. 3x(x² - 4) #This is not complete because x squared minus 4 is a difference of squares.
=B. 3x(x - 2)(x + 2) #Correct. Factor out 3x, then factor x squared minus 4.
~C. 3(x - 2)(x + 2) #This is missing the factor x.
~D. x(3x - 12) #This is not fully factored and does not preserve the cubic term correctly.
}


::ALG2_U01_L03_Q20::
Question ID: ALG2_U01_L03_Q20
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.AE.1; MLA.A2.AE.5
Question:
Which statement best explains why (x - 4)(x + 4) is equivalent to x² - 16?
{
~A. The constants add to 0, so the expression equals x squared. #This ignores the constant product.
~B. The expression is equivalent because 4 plus 4 equals 8. #The sum of the constants does not verify the product.
~C. It is equivalent only when x equals 0. #Equivalent expressions match for every allowed input, not just one value.
=D. Multiplying gives x² + 4x - 4x - 16, and the middle terms cancel. #Correct. The product simplifies to x squared - 16.
}

